Pages = {385-394},
Month = {MAY-AUG},
Abstract = {This article proposes a model of environmental interpretation in which
   the ecosystem, the cultural system, and the ethics of least harm
   interact. The research is based on a documentary methodology with a
   critical approach, aimed at designing the environmental interpretation
   model. The review articulates a deconstruction of (hegemonic) economic
   views on nature and the observations of an interdisciplinary group
   constituted, at the institutional level, as a co-research team that
   reflected on the environmentalization of the curriculum at the
   Pedagogical and Technological University of Colombia. A central aspect
   of the proposal is the concept of ``ethics of least harm,{''} viewed
   from an ecosophical praxis, which assumes the relationship between
   ecosystems and the cultural system from an ethical, aesthetic, and
   political perspective, and which promotes processes of self-reflection
   in individuals about their connection with the rest of nature. The
   originality of the work lies in the recognition of the implications of
   the model, that is, in the anticipation of a whole social transformation
   aligned with the common good in the environmental dimension. It also
   recognizes that this transformation can only take place through a change
   in awareness of interdependent subjectivities, which requires
   self-responsible decisions and the activation of identities based on the
   link with the territory. In the long term, the aim is to enable human
   intervention and the resulting damage to to be and for this assessment
   to new contributions to deterioration.},
